Renegotiate while old surfaces are still referenced

A client that changes resolution or bit depth creates the new surfaces
before it lets go of the old ones: their frames are still queued for
display, or held as references, when the new sequence starts. With the
queues owned by those surfaces (0009 keeps them alive until the last one
goes), the S_FMT for the new geometry met EBUSY, surface creation failed,
and the client fell back to software. Measured 2026-10-01 with ffmpeg 7.1.5:
VP9 and HEVC both renegotiate this way, and both dropped to software at the
first change.

With capture memory owned by the surfaces (0018) the queues can be let go
of instead. When surfaces are created for a different format, size or depth
while no context is live, and the device's capture memory is DMABUF:

- both queues are stopped and released (REQBUFS 0). The OUTPUT buffers are
  MMAP and still mapped by their surfaces; vb2 orphans them, and the
  surfaces unmap them when destroyed;
- the device's queue generation is bumped, which retires every surface
  created before;
- the existing S_FMT path runs, unchanged.

A retired surface keeps its own dma-buf and a record of its own format, so
it can still be synced, read back and exported. Readback needed two more
changes, both found by ffmpeg's hwdownload of frames decoded before the
change:

- vaCreateImage laid every image out with the queue's current pitch and
  refused one bigger than the queue's picture. After a renegotiation the
  queue describes the NEW stream, so an image for an old frame either got
  the wrong pitch (VP9 640x360 -> 1280x720) or was refused outright (HEVC
  640x480 -> 320x240). It now uses the queue's layout only when that holds
  the image at the image's own depth, and a plain layout otherwise.
- The surface -> image copy goes row by row when the two pitches differ.
  vaDeriveImage lays its image out from the surface itself. The refusal of
  an image larger than the picture behind it -- the scaled-decode guard --
  moves from vaCreateImage to the copy, where the surface is known. It is refused as a decode
target, including as AV1's separate display surface: its queue slot now
belongs to another configuration. Destroying it touches no queue, and it no
longer counts as keeping the device in use.

Destroying surfaces now happens in mid-stream, from whichever thread lets go
of the last old frame -- ffmpeg's HEVC frame threads do -- and
RequestDestroySurfaces used to switch the active device to every idle
decoder it checked and leave the last one active. The decode running on
another thread then issued its ioctls on the AV1 core's descriptors
(QUERYBUF EINVAL on the wrong fd, a failed surface, and a vaCreateImage that
found no format). It now reads each idle device's parked state without
switching, switches only to release, and restores the active device.

MMAP capture keeps the previous behaviour (EBUSY, software fallback), and a
same-geometry context re-creation -- every seek and loop, 0009's case --
still keeps both the queues and the surfaces.

Not covered: AV1 through ffmpeg 7.1 never asks. libavcodec's AV1 decoder
keeps its hwaccel across a new sequence header whenever the hardware pixel
format is still offered, for size and depth changes alike, so no new
surfaces are ever requested.

